      "id": "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6",
      "title": "Computing the Rate of Change of a Multivariable Function at a Point Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e445a1aa-802d-5ede-9576-5e94dad51f59",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=- \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\sqrt{t},- 3 t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(- \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(- \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$, $y=- 3 t$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨- \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} - - 3 t \\right)},\\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} - - 3 t \\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨- \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)},\\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}},-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨- \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)},\\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}},-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(- \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2 \\sqrt{t}}\\right)+\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(-3\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(- 6 \\sqrt{t} - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{t} + 3 t \\right)}}{2 \\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(1))] & =\\frac{\\left(- 6 \\sqrt{(1)} - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(\\sqrt{(1)} + 3 (1) \\right)}}{2 \\sqrt{(1)}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{7 \\cos{\\left(4 \\right)}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
