    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2952890d-c634-5895-9a06-9df9274559e7",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=2 x^{3} - 3 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)},- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 x^{3} - 3 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 x^{3} - 3 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6 x^{2},- 6 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\\cdot ⟨3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)},2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(54 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(12 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =6 \\left(27 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 4\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right))] & =6 \\left(27 \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} + 4\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{81}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right)+\\left(-6\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\sqrt{3}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{243}{4}- 6 \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{243}{4} - 6 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
