    "question": {
      ":question/id": "316b130e-724c-5a2a-bc18-033111415c7c",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=x^{3} - 3 y^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)=⟨\\sin{\\left(t \\right)},- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} - 3 y^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} - 3 y^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3 x^{2},- 6 y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$ into the above to find $∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =⟨3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},18 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(- 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =∇f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)},18 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}⟩\\cdot ⟨\\cos{\\left(t \\right)},3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}⟩ \\\\  & =\\left(3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)+\\left(18 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 18\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right))] & =3 \\left(\\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} + 18\\right) \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\left(\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right) \\right)} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{9}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\left(-9\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =- \\frac{9}{8}- \\frac{27 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{27 \\sqrt{3}}{2} - \\frac{9}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
